00:15:28.570 --> 00:15:31.870
The input function, it will
read the data in batches

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:34.250
and you can set it to
shuffle your training data.

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:43.960
So just a reminder, this
is where iteration 3 is.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.780
Everything is almost in
Google Cloud Platform,

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:50.090
except for the beginning part,
which is the original database.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.150
And now Austin will
take us through how

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:53.740
he moved that to BigQuery.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.740
AUSTIN BENNETT:
Thank you, Salma.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:00.670
OK, cool.

00:16:00.670 --> 00:16:04.140
So oh, I meant to switch
the order of these slides.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:08.830
OK, so BigQuery is great.

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:10.510
I mean I talked
about a trade-off

00:16:10.510 --> 00:16:17.620
before between a data
lake and an MPP database.

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:22.900
Realistically, BigQuery gives
you the best of both worlds.

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:24.580
Curating data and
needing to think

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:29.147
about the files on a
storage system is a pain

00:16:29.147 --> 00:16:30.230
and I don't want to do it.

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:33.340
And I'm glad there's a
managed service for it.

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:36.620
I can update data and
not think about things.

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:41.260
It's as performant as
anything else I've seen.

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:44.260
I haven't needed to
do a ton with it yet,

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:48.520
but the ability to share data
across different organizations,

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:50.920
across projects is
super straightforward.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:55.660
And I didn't pay attention
enough earlier today as to

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:58.180
whether or not the BigQuery
Read API was talked about.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:02.560
But it's super cool,
gives us direct access

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:07.450
to the underlying
storage, for then

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:12.339
letting Dataflow, Beam jobs,
or Spark, et cetera; access

00:17:12.339 --> 00:17:13.480
that data directly.

00:17:16.085 --> 00:17:20.440
A great way to try
stuff out also,

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:22.810
which I wish would have
existed when I first

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:25.869
got started, is there's
a transfer service

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:30.100
that input a few
parameters in the UI, which

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:31.390
I prefer to not use UIs.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:34.300
But this will get you
moving really quick.

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:36.820
In our case, we moved
a few terabytes over,

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:41.290
and saw that
performance was great.

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:44.710
Given that I just gave you a
great sales pitch on how great

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:46.750
it is, I also like--

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:49.160
everything isn't all
roses all the time.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:49.660
Right?

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:55.670
So I ran out of resources,
even on BigQuery

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.190
in one specific instance.

00:17:57.190 --> 00:18:00.520
Although oh well, talking
with the BigQuery team,

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:03.070
eventually we were able to
do this sort of query which

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:05.020
we were able to do elsewhere.

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:08.620
They fixed things, more
or less, and then this

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:11.540
became not an issue.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:16.960
So ML can't be successful
without ingesting and moving

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:17.730
data around.

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:22.360
So I want to talk
about a few ways batch

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:25.780
ingests from various places,
third party APIs that we

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:30.940
may hit and stream APIs,
and using some various open

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:32.580
source or at least
managed tools.

00:18:36.460 --> 00:18:40.970
Also containerization
is pretty cool.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.340
A lot of our stuff
has been orchestrated

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:47.060
from a central
virtual machine, which

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:49.730
although has worked
very well, also

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:59.330
has its problems for scheduling,
resource conflicts, and package

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:02.150
issues and versions.

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:04.220
Those sorts of
things go away when

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:09.170
we adopt these sorts of
containerization practices,

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:11.930
which we've been
working towards.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:17.010
Many of our batch ingests
are patterns like this.

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:18.960
And also I guess
the point I'm going

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:20.600
to try to get
across in this talk

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:24.410
too is multi-cloud
is totally doable.

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:29.480
Use the great tools
where they exist

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.890
or within the confines
of your organization.

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.390
If people say you need
to use something, don't--

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:38.130
it's not all or nothing.

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.850
So many of our workflows are--

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.380
data gets dropped
into S3, for instance.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.190
This is a very simplified
workflow of one,

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:49.190
say, Spark job that
transforms stuff back into S3.

00:19:49.190 --> 00:19:52.460
To do things with it, for
instance, just take that output

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:55.040
and copy it over using
the transfer service

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:57.420
or gsutil or whatnot.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:59.160
And then it's in Cloud Storage.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:01.490
Another way that
we've played with

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:05.140
is on data ingest in
the first place into S3,

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:06.470
copy it over to Cloud Storage.

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:11.387
Write that Beam or Dataflow job,
get it over to Cloud Storage.

00:20:11.387 --> 00:20:13.220
Especially once it's
there and because we're

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:15.620
talking about batch, it's
straightforward to get it

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:17.840
into BigQuery.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:26.210
Also Dataflow and Beam can
go straight into BigQuery.

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:32.190
And then I put "poof" because
I think I haven't tried it yet,

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:36.140
but I think that there's
zero problem having

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:38.390
Dataflow and Beam
read-write from S3,

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.930
and right there, the
things exist to do it.

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:42.890
But I haven't tried it.

00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:46.860
But that seems like a great way
to simplify my architecture.

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:49.220
Another bit on batch
ingest, we have

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:50.900
a bunch of third-party
data providers

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.100
that we get data from.

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:56.810
So hit the API in
the first iteration.

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:59.720
GKE and a container was
managing everything.

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:02.930
So it would go to BigQuery,
get say the last time

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:06.470
stamp of things, go back to the
API and get the data it needs,

00:21:06.470 --> 00:21:11.690
write to storage, and then
we'd use a Dataflow and Beam

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:17.000
job to write things to, say,
BigQuery and Google Storage.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:19.730
Things got a little
more simplified

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:21.890
when adding Airflow
to the mix, which

00:21:21.890 --> 00:21:26.900
then let the orchestration
be in the Composer Airflow

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:32.930
layer, and smaller containers,
and discrete services.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:38.120
And then we were able
to reduce query costs

00:21:38.120 --> 00:21:42.320
from keeping the metadata
itself in Firestore, rather

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:48.890
than in BigQuery and essentially
using Firestore as a metadata

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:53.690
store of the last time stamp, so
just a quick key value lookup.

00:21:53.690 --> 00:21:58.790
Streaming data ingests, we have
processes that look like this.

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:04.250
NGINX and LogStash, on premise
endpoints, stream to Kafka,

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:05.810
go to LogStash.

00:22:05.810 --> 00:22:09.260
That winds up getting
bashed and written to S3

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:11.390
and loaded into Redshift.

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:14.270
To support our other fantastic
groups and colleagues

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:17.510
we work with, we then wound up
adopting more of the data lake

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:20.810
model, unloading
things out of Redshift

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:22.880
using, say, Spark;
then writing that

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:28.160
back into S3 for columnar bits.

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:31.910
Since we absolutely
have to support S3,

00:22:31.910 --> 00:22:35.270
Dataflow and Beam though
can read-write from Kafka

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:39.050
and are able to write to
these other bits first.

00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:42.020
Streaming data,
we're still toying

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:44.570
with whether we're going
to get enough value out

00:22:44.570 --> 00:22:47.990
of Bigtable itself to let
BigQuery read from Bigtable,

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:54.180
or write to both, or if
BigQuery is going to be itself.

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:59.450
And although Dataflow and Beam
can talk to our Kafka clusters

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:01.730
directly, we went
ahead and just jumped

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:04.730
all the way to having NGINX
and LogStash forward write

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:11.000
to Pub/Sub, because with the
eventual goal being let's

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:13.880
get our on premise
infrastructure into the cloud,

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:17.210
and it looks like we're leaning
towards using Cloud Endpoints.

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:20.660
And Cloud Functions,
although we're first

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:25.790
proving out the rest of
the pipeline that's here.

00:23:25.790 --> 00:23:28.610
Big fan of things Beam
and giving a shout out

00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:33.860
to this session on Thursday
of my colleagues there,

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:38.750
11:40 to 12:30 Stream
Analytics stuff.

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:42.875
And then Salma's going to
share more on our modeling.

00:23:42.875 --> 00:23:43.708
SALMA RIAZI: Thanks.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:54.180
So now that we have data in
BigQuery, thank you Austin

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:57.460
for putting it in BigQuery,
everything is in the query now.

00:23:57.460 --> 00:24:00.960
So now I start from BigQuery.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:02.910
But again, as Austin
mentioned earlier,

00:24:02.910 --> 00:24:05.100
we were at Next
last year, and we

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:08.070
learned about all these
new tools like Dataflow,

00:24:08.070 --> 00:24:13.470
tf.Transform that are great for
preprocessing big scale data.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:16.140
So I wanted to give
those a try, and I

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:19.830
found these courses on Coursera
that are really helpful.

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:23.250
And Datalab is a great tool to
follow the courses on Coursera

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:25.470
and learn tf.Transform.

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:28.260
So this time I started
from BigQuery, then

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:29.790
did the preprocessing
on Datalab.

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:32.160
But with preprocessing
that means

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:35.250
submitting a job to Dataflow.

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:38.520
And then I saved the
preprocessed data

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:44.040
on Google Storage, trained
the same model on CMLE,

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:48.990
predicted on CMLE, and
brought it to BigQuery.

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:53.290
But this time no preprocess for
predictions, so that's great.

00:24:56.570 --> 00:25:00.900
Here is a learning example
of tf.Transform on Datalab.

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.570
In case you guys
are interested, you

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:05.010
can follow it and
troubleshoot your code.

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:09.280
It's interactive and it's easy
to follow examples, serverless

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:10.500
and customizable.

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:16.970
So here's our final
iteration, which

00:25:16.970 --> 00:25:19.100
is much shorter
than the beginning,

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:22.565
and scalable, and serverless.

00:25:22.565 --> 00:25:27.740
We start from BigQuery, do
the preprocessing on Dataflow,

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:32.100
save the tfrecord
files on Cloud Storage,

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:35.220
do that training and
predictions on CMLE

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:38.600
and write it to BigQuery.

00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:41.510
Here we solved
all these problems

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:46.400
of scale, extra packages,
and no more pickle files.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:48.590
There is no preprocessing
for predictions,

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:51.770
because tf.Transform
writes the graph.

00:25:51.770 --> 00:25:53.780
And you can point your
model to the graph

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:56.120
of the output of
the tf.Transform,

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:59.850
and it will automatically
preprocess it for you.

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:03.620
Here is an example of
Beam pipeline that I used.

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:06.320
First I read the
data from BigQuery,

00:26:06.320 --> 00:26:08.830
no more unloading
data from a database.

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:12.410
It just reads it
straight from there.

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:15.530
Second step is grouping
by user, which is just

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:17.270
reordering the elements.

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:19.420
Then I insert zeros and reshape.

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:23.330
I'm going to get into the
details in the upcoming slides.

00:26:23.330 --> 00:26:26.360
And then I scale and encode
using the tf.Transform

00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:27.110
functions.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:32.010
And then finally I write
it to a tfrecord file.

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:34.510
These are the first three steps.

00:26:34.510 --> 00:26:38.730
The first function is just a
query, just select a query.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:41.430
The second one is
data validation.

00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:44.940
Here I'm checking to see if
days greater and equal to zero,

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:48.060
but it could be anything.

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:52.794
The third step is
collect users, which

00:26:52.794 --> 00:26:59.470
it reorders the input elements,
so it can group by later.

00:26:59.470 --> 00:27:02.890
The fourth step is the write
format, NumPy operation

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:05.950
function, which is just
a normal NumPy function.

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:10.480
And the fifth step is where the
encoding and scaling happens.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:15.670
So after the fifth step,
the tf.Transform graph

00:27:15.670 --> 00:27:17.830
gets written to
your bucket, where

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:20.840
you can point your model to.

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:23.510
Here's a Dataflow example.

00:27:23.510 --> 00:27:25.100
I'm going to show
you a demo later.

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:28.670
But just in case,
here's an example.

00:27:28.670 --> 00:27:32.720
You can follow each step
and see where you are.

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:35.700
You can see your job
description and summary.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:40.520
And on the right middle pane,
you can see the auto scaling.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:44.420
So here I needed 70
workers for 10 minutes.

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:47.240
It auto scales to 70
and then back down.

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:50.760
And my job was
done in 15 minutes.

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:52.560
So now I'm going to
move to the demo.

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:06.240
All right, so there's
these two files

00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:09.180
that are very similar
to what I showed you.

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:17.110
I'm going to run it
from the terminal,

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:19.964
and hope that it will work.

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:32.810
So here's the GCP
platform, the homepage.

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:36.630
Here you can find the
Compute Engine, ML engine;

00:28:36.630 --> 00:28:39.190
all these tools that I just
talked about, BigQuery.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:42.590
But I'm interested in Dataflow.

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:47.070
And one of them has kicked off.

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:49.157
The graph is still
being analyzed.

00:28:49.157 --> 00:28:50.490
So we have to wait a little bit.

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:04.630
OK.

00:29:04.630 --> 00:29:07.640
So here you can follow
what's happening.

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.490
The first step is the BQImport.

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:12.020
And it didn't run.

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:15.040
So in order to check why, you
can always click on the step

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:16.120
and click on the logs.

00:29:18.890 --> 00:29:23.540
And it will give you the
error, which here it says,

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:27.450
BigQuery execution failed
because unrecognized name.

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:31.600
I just put an
unrecognized name there.

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:33.550
So you can see the error.

00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:40.000
This is the second one,
which is exactly like the one

00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:42.160
I showed you before.

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:46.480
You can see the
steps that's running.

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:49.090
I'm running it for my
training data and also

00:29:49.090 --> 00:29:50.890
the validation data.

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:52.540
And they all get saved.

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:57.850
Here is where the transform
function gets saved.

00:29:57.850 --> 00:30:02.400
And this is where the
transform happens.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.140
The auto-scaling is kicking off.

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:08.280
Here you can see the job
information, and the resource

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:11.790
metrics, and the
pipeline options.

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:15.030
You can always submit
a setup file with it

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:18.070
to specify what you want.

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:19.420
Here's the auto scaling.

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:20.970
The current workers are 0.

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:23.565
But it wants to be 70.

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:28.640
If we weight a little
bit, the current workers

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:31.950
will go up to 70, and then back
down, and the job will be done.

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:35.480
This is the same job that I
showed you the picture before.

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:39.110
So we don't have
to wait 15 minutes.

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:40.990
So I'm going to go
back to the slides now.

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:54.060
All right, so in the
model the only thing

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:58.140
that you need to change is
the serving input function.

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:03.790
Where you point it to the
tf.Transform graph output.

00:31:03.790 --> 00:31:05.080
This is how it happens.

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:09.460
And then the lower is
the predictions output,

00:31:09.460 --> 00:31:12.130
where there's viewer
ID, probabilities,

00:31:12.130 --> 00:31:14.720
and prediction classes.

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:17.800
So now that we have all the
pieces of the puzzle together,

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:21.250
it would be great if we could
have this automated daily,

00:31:21.250 --> 00:31:24.190
so we could have daily
