We're doing batch predictions.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:44.540
One of the more straightforward
ways of doing it

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:50.660
is unload from BigQuery to get
those records in new line JSON

00:31:50.660 --> 00:31:52.560
format.

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:55.750
In this case, we
used Beam Dataflow.

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:58.600
Write those to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:31:58.600 --> 00:32:06.140
A second step being hit the
ml-engine predict service, read

00:32:06.140 --> 00:32:08.660
those files from Cloud
Storage, write them back

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:11.750
to Cloud Storage, and
then just for what we've

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:15.950
been doing those batch results
are just going into BigQuery,

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:17.760
so we can do things with them.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:20.720
So it's a three-step process.

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:23.900
To highlight what this
is, we have our Beam job,

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:26.540
which is called extract.py.

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:29.600
The documentation is super
straightforward on how

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:35.390
to use Beam Dataflow
to do some things.

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:39.740
Composer Airflow less
so, for these operators,

00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:43.070
I'm really hoping they wind
up getting accepted for--

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:46.550
I forget, if it's summer
or season of docs--

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:50.570
where then there should
be some resources helping

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:53.330
even the open source projects
get some documentation going.

00:32:53.330 --> 00:33:00.980
But you can take more or
less the Python command line

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:04.460
and translate that into
what's needed for Airflow.

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:08.390
This is for the
Dataflow operator.

00:33:08.390 --> 00:33:10.370
Equivalently, hey,
we see a whole lot

00:33:10.370 --> 00:33:14.570
of variables we're using and
using gcloud ml-engine job

00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:18.350
submit, again
documentation really great.

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:21.620
And here with the ml-engine
batch prediction operator,

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:24.980
you can see I tried to make
this pretty straightforward,

00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:28.970
where it's pretty much
plug-and-play here.

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:35.120
And the same goes for the BQ
command line tool and the load

00:33:35.120 --> 00:33:39.440
command; a little bit more
to it, but also again,

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:41.790
pretty straight forward.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:46.160
So that's how we weaved
these things together.

00:33:46.160 --> 00:33:50.390
Yes, they can be Cron jobs
for those sort of command line

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:51.260
tools that we saw.

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:56.030
But Airflow Composer gives us a
lot more visibility into stuff.

00:33:56.030 --> 00:33:57.650
And also, hey,
let's learn these,

00:33:57.650 --> 00:34:02.480
because those lessons get
applied all over the place,

00:34:02.480 --> 00:34:07.130
like we saw earlier for
moving batch data around.

00:34:07.130 --> 00:34:12.960
OK, so, so far summary, we've
seen how we slowly built

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:15.420
onto our eventual solution.

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:19.760
The real goal was how can
we have in our toolset

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:24.780
the best scalable solution that
we can work on even with, say,

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:27.440
our 12 gigabyte
data sets when we

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:30.679
need to work with our
mini terabyte data sets,

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:32.540
and anything in between.

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:34.940
But hopefully as
you've seen, you

00:34:34.940 --> 00:34:39.650
